      "rawOutput": "Looking at this problem, I need to find a set B that minimizes |B|²/v where v is the largest integer such that all integers 1,2,...,v appear as pairwise differences.\n\nThis is related to the concept of Sidon sets or B_h sequences in additive combinatorics. I want to maximize the coverage v while keeping the set size |B| as small as possible.\n\nLet me construct a set systematically. I'll start with a Singer difference set approach, which is known to give good difference coverage.\n\nFor a Singer difference set of order q, we can use elements of the form i·q + j where we carefully select the indices. However, let me try a more direct greedy approach optimized for this specific objective.\n\nI'll build a set by including numbers that maximize new differences while minimizing set size growth:\n\nStarting with {0}, I'll add elements that create the most new consecutive differences:\n- 0: gives no differences yet\n- 1: gives difference 1\n- 3: gives differences 2,3 (with 0,1)\n- 6: gives differences 3,5,6 (with 0,1,3) - but 3 already exists, so adds 5,6\n- 13: gives several new differences\n- Continue this pattern...\n\nLet me construct a well-known good difference set:\n\n{0, 1, 3, 7, 12, 20, 30, 44, 65, 80, 96, 122, 150, 185, 204, 252, 290, 316, 397, 480}\n\nBut let me try a more systematic approach using a quadratic residue pattern which often gives good results:\n\n```json\n{\"set\": [0, 1, 4, 9, 11, 16, 19, 25, 29, 36, 41, 44, 49, 56, 61, 64, 71, 76, 81, 89, 100, 104, 109, 116, 121, 124, 131, 139, 144, 149, 156, 164, 169, 176, 181, 196, 201, 209, 216, 225, 229, 236, 241, 244, 249, 256, 264, 289, 324, 361, 400, 441, 484, 529, 576, 625, 676, 729, 784, 841, 900, 961]}\n```"
